import{_ as a,o as t,c as o,a6 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BTpfZnOf.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"JAX runtime and derivatives","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"jax-runtime.md","filePath":"jax-runtime.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"jax-runtime.md"};function s(n,e,d,c,l,h){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="JAX-runtime-and-derivatives" tabindex="-1">JAX runtime and derivatives <a class="header-anchor" href="#JAX-runtime-and-derivatives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;JAX runtime and derivatives {#JAX-runtime-and-derivatives}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This guide continues <a href="./jax">JAX source</a>: runtime checks on accelerators, chains sampled as one batch, derivative rules in emitted modules and stable rewrites. <a href="./jax-ode">JAX ODE solves</a> covers the ODE bridges.</p><h2 id="Accelerator-runtime-checks" tabindex="-1">Accelerator runtime checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#Accelerator-runtime-checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Accelerator runtime checks {#Accelerator-runtime-checks}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Data-dependent checks use <code>jax.debug.callback</code>: their predicates are computed on the numerical device and checked on the host. JAX needs a local CPU device for these callbacks, even for valid inputs on CUDA. Start the Python process with <code>JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu</code>; <code>JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda</code> alone fails when a runtime check executes. CPU-only execution remains supported.</p><p>An explicit platform list makes CUDA initialization mandatory and CUDA the default; it does not silently fall back to CPU if CUDA initialization fails. For a GPU-required consumer, select <code>gpu = jax.devices(&quot;gpu&quot;)[0]</code>, place numerical inputs with <code>jax.device_put(value, gpu)</code>, and verify result and gradient arrays with <code>array.devices() == {gpu}</code>. Evaluate inside <code>with jax.default_device(gpu):</code> so newly created constants use that device too. Numerical function results, including constant terms and numeric leaves of tuple/dict results, are JAX arrays with the emitted dtype. <code>prepare_data</code>, <code>dimension</code>, <code>parameters</code> and module metadata remain host values. The host validates the check predicates; it does not evaluate the numerical kernel. Call <code>jax.block_until_ready(result)</code> and <code>jax.effects_barrier()</code> when checking completion and callback errors. Host callbacks incur transfer and synchronization costs; <code>prepare_data</code> also runs on the host.</p><p>See JAX&#39;s <a href="https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/external-callbacks.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">callback semantics</a> and <a href="https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/config_options.html#platforms" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">platform configuration</a>. <code>python benchmark/jax_runtime_checks.py</code> verifies the helper&#39;s values, gradients, device placement, invalid-input rejection and missing-GPU failure with a CUDA-enabled JAX installation.</p><h2 id="Selected-station-or-subject-densities" tabindex="-1">Selected station or subject densities <a class="header-anchor" href="#Selected-station-or-subject-densities" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Selected station or subject densities {#Selected-station-or-subject-densities}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For station or subject minibatches, use <code>jax_source(built, bound, observations; by, partitioned, global_factors, unit_factors, expected_layout)</code>. Write <code>out.source</code> and send <code>out.partition_data</code> with the model data. In Python, <code>q = model.prepare_partition(p, partition_data)</code> takes the result of <code>p = model.prepare_data(**raw)</code>. <code>model.global_log_density(u, **q)</code> sums the shared events. <code>model.unit_log_density(u, label, **q)</code> sums that label&#39;s observations and assigned prior and Jacobian events at its own full packed point. Both support <code>jax.jit</code>, <code>jax.vmap</code> and reverse differentiation. A complete assignment at one point reconstructs <code>model.log_density(u, **p)</code>. An unsupported indexed primitive or effect raises during tracing.</p><h2 id="Chains-under-vmap" tabindex="-1">Chains under <code>vmap</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#Chains-under-vmap" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chains under `vmap` {#Chains-under-vmap}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A sampler that advances several chains as one batch, <code>jax.vmap</code> over its transition (BlackJAX&#39;s NUTS, for example), runs each of its while loops until the last chain is done. JAX runs a batched loop&#39;s body for every lane and keeps the old carry where a lane&#39;s loop has ended, so a chain that is done is still evaluated on every later step of the batch, and the result is discarded. In NUTS such a chain stays at the state where it stopped: after a divergence, every remaining leapfrog step of the batch evaluates the density one step past the divergent point, and a chain that is done can even keep an inner loop running after every live chain has left it.</p><p>This costs nothing extra when a density costs the same everywhere. It does when the cost depends on the position, as an ODE solve&#39;s does (<a href="./jax-ode">JAX ODE solves</a>): past a divergence the system can be stiff, and one lane&#39;s solve then holds every call of the batch. In a four-chain warm-up on CUDA of a density with a five-state solve in each of 24 plate cells (float64, tolerances <code>1e-8</code>, at most 100,000 steps per output), one chain diverged after 5 of 127 leapfrog steps. Its remaining evaluations took ten times the solver attempts of each running chain&#39;s, and the transition took 25 s where the others took about 4 s. At a saved proposal whose solves exhaust the step budget, one value and gradient takes 16.7 s on the card and 0.11 s on one CPU core.</p><p>Guard the sampler, not the model: give a chain that is done a state it has already evaluated. In BlackJAX 1.7.1 that is two changes in <code>blackjax.mcmc.trajectory</code>. In <code>dynamic_progressive_integration</code>&#39;s loop body, integrate from the subtree&#39;s initial state instead of <code>trajectory.rightmost_state</code> when the lane had already diverged, terminated or taken its steps. In <code>dynamic_multiplicative_expansion</code>&#39;s, start a finished lane&#39;s subtree at the transition&#39;s initial state with zero steps. Only discarded results change. Compiled to round alike, the guarded and unguarded samplers gave bitwise identical draws on Neal&#39;s funnel, with 420 instead of 2,694 evaluations far out in it (<code>|v| &gt; 6</code>).</p><h2 id="Derivative-rules" tabindex="-1">Derivative rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#Derivative-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Derivative rules {#Derivative-rules}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For <code>scalar_derivative_rule</code>, the module generates a <code>jax.custom_jvp</code> adapter from the rule&#39;s mathematical graph. It selects the primal and named partials for the active arguments, then applies the scalar chain rule. An inactive argument&#39;s partial is not contracted with a numeric zero. Calls share their generated helpers, including a joint recipe that serves several activity patterns. Host data preparation uses the primal cut.</p><p>General <code>derivative_rule</code> calls generate adapters from the authored forward branch and activity-selected staged reverse branches. Ordinary <code>jax.grad</code> and <code>jax.value_and_grad</code> compute the joint primal and residuals once, then contract the selected reverse cut. JAX does not differentiate the primal&#39;s convergence loop in place of the supplied derivative. Forward mode uses the authored forward branch; a missing branch or differentiation of a declared data-only input raises. Batching maps whole calls and retains each lane&#39;s lazy branches. These adapters use <code>jax.experimental.hijax.HiPrim</code> and are tested with JAX 0.12.0.1. Only modules containing a general rule import that API.</p><p>Ragged array ports carry their logical lengths through primal, forward and reverse cuts. Lengths have zero tangent; padded cotangents do not contribute to upstream gradients. Runtime checks inside a rule execute only for active calls, including rules reached through a lazy branch or an empty plate. Rules containing array regions retain their source plans as build-time metadata. Native invocation reads only the compiled body and its concrete execution tables.</p><p>The incomplete-beta continued fraction used by distribution tails supplies its value and shape/argument partials through such a shared graph. A first JAX gradient retains those partials rather than a reverse tape of all 300 fraction iterations. The primal and partials use fixed loops, so their higher derivatives remain available through ordinary JAX differentiation.</p><h2 id="Stable-rewrites" tabindex="-1">Stable rewrites <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stable-rewrites" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stable rewrites {#Stable-rewrites}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Unless <code>simplify = false</code>, each emitted recipe computes numerically fragile compositions in the stable form native preparation computes, together with the statements that compute its inputs, also from a lazy arm or a plate&#39;s cell: <code>log(exp(u))</code> is emitted as <code>u</code>, and <code>log1p(-p)</code> of <code>p = logistic(u)</code> reads <code>u</code>, so the value and <code>jax.grad</code> stay finite where <code>exp(u)</code> overflows. A law&#39;s term on a value computed outside the law (<code>Normal(0, s)</code>&#39;s <code>log(s)</code> of <code>s = exp(a)</code>) is still translated as written: the law&#39;s endpoint is translated from its compiled lowered code, which the rewrites do not read yet.</p>',19)])])}const m=a(i,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
